'''''Balboa v. Riera i Sola''''' (also known as the '''''El Desig'' obscenity trial''') was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which the government prosecuted the filmmaker [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]] under the [[Public Decency Act]] of 1976 for the production and distribution of ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' ("The Desire"), a feminist film that depicted female sexuality, same-sex attraction, and abortion. The trial, which lasted from March to September 1988 before the Sant Cristòfor High Court, became a cause célèbre that galvanised the underground film community, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups, and drew the mainstream film industry into a public confrontation with the government over the scope of artistic freedom under the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The court's acquittal of Riera i Sola and its ruling that the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was unconstitutionally vague effectively gutted the statute and established a precedent protecting artistic expression from obscenity prosecution in Balboa.

== Background ==

{{main|El Desig (film)}}

''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' was the second film of [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]], a feminist filmmaker born in 1948 in [[Sant Bart]] who had moved to [[Sant Cristòfor]] in the early 1970s and had worked as a scriptwriter for [[Cinema Lliure]] before leaving the mainstream studio to make films that addressed subjects the mainstream would not finance. Her first film, ''[[Les Dones del Port (film)|Les Dones del Port]]'' (1981), was a documentary about the women who worked in the harbour district's informal economy, shot on a minimal budget and screened at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]], a community arts space in the harbour-front district that had established a regular screening programme in 1985.

''El Desig'' was produced outside the mainstream studio system and was financed through personal funds and small contributions from feminist organisations and individual supporters. The film depicted the sexual and economic lives of three women in Sant Cristòfor during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of the 1980s. Its explicit content — which included depictions of female desire, same-sex attraction, and abortion — was the first frank portrayal of female sexuality in a Balboan film and was regarded by the government as a violation of the [[Public Decency Act]].

=== The Public Decency Act of 1976 ===

The [[Public Decency Act]] was enacted in 1976 during the administration of President [[Miquel Torrent i Alvarado]] as part of a broader package of social legislation aimed at the conservative interior provinces. The Act prohibited the production, distribution, and exhibition of "obscene material," defined as material that "offends public morality by depicting sexual conduct in a manner that is gratuitous or without artistic, scientific, or educational merit." The Act had been passed at the request of interior provincial assemblies, particularly those of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], where conservative social norms and the influence of the [[Balboan Catholic Church]] were strongest. The Act had not previously been used to prosecute a film, and its application to ''El Desig'' was its first significant test in a judicial proceeding.

== Prosecution ==

The film was screened at the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre in late 1987. The screening was attended by approximately 200 people, including members of the underground film community, feminist organisations, and the Sant Cristòfor cultural press. A report of the screening was forwarded to the Ministry of Justice by an official in the Sant Cristòfor municipal government, whose identity has not been publicly confirmed. The Ministry of Justice referred the matter to the prosecutor's office, and in January 1988 the government initiated prosecution under the Public Decency Act.

The prosecution was led by [[Francesc Bauzá i Mas]], a deputy prosecutor from the Ministry of Justice. The charges were brought against Riera i Sola as the film's director and producer, and against the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre as the venue where the film had been exhibited. The Cultural Centre charges were subsequently dropped after the Centre's management demonstrated that the screening had been conducted under its regular programming schedule and that the Centre had not been involved in the film's production.

The prosecution argued that the film's depiction of sexuality was gratuitous and that its distribution constituted a violation of the Public Decency Act's provisions against the dissemination of obscene material. Bauzá i Mas argued that the film's scenes of sexual conduct, including its depiction of female desire and same-sex attraction, served no narrative or artistic purpose that could not have been achieved through less explicit means, and that the film's inclusion of an abortion scene was designed to provoke rather than to inform. The prosecution called two witnesses: a professor of literature from the [[Universitat de Sant Cristòfor]] who testified that the film's artistic merit was marginal, and a representative of the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] who testified that the film's content was contrary to the moral values of the Balboan interior.

== Defence ==

The defence was led by the [[Balboan Civil Liberties Union]] (BCLU), a civil rights organisation that had been established during the mandate period to advocate for the enforcement of the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The lead defence attorney was [[Magda Vives i Cardó]], the BCLU's legal director. The defence argued that the film was a legitimate artistic expression protected by the Twelve Guarantees' provisions on freedom of expression and that the Public Decency Act was an unconstitutional restriction on artistic freedom.

Vives i Cardó argued that the film's explicit content was integral to its artistic purpose: the film's depiction of female sexuality was not gratuitous but was the subject of the film itself, and the film's exploration of the private lives of its characters was a legitimate artistic endeavour that the Twelve Guarantees' protections for freedom of expression were designed to shield. The defence called three witnesses: [[Jordi Molins i Castell]], the mainstream satirical filmmaker, who testified that the film's artistic merit was evident and that the prosecution's standard for "gratuitous" content, if accepted, would endanger a wide range of Balboan films; [[Kwame Rovira]], the Black Balboan subcultural filmmaker, who testified that the underground film community had been producing films on similar subjects for years without prosecution and that the government's selective prosecution of ''El Desig'' was politically motivated; and a professor of constitutional law from the [[Balboan Jesuit University]] who testified that the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was unconstitutionally vague under the Twelve Guarantees.

== The trial ==

The trial began on 14 March 1988 before the Sant Cristòfor High Court, Criminal Division. The presiding judge was [[Jordi Tarradellas i Bayarri]]. The trial lasted approximately six months, with intermittent hearings, and concluded on 22 September 1988 with the court's ruling.

The proceedings were conducted in Volisanian, the official language of Balboa. Riera i Sola was present throughout the trial and testified in her own defence. The courtroom, which had a capacity of approximately 80, was filled to capacity for every hearing session, with overflow crowds gathered outside the courthouse.

== Media coverage and public reaction ==

The trial generated the most intensive media coverage of any court case in Balboa since reunification. The Sant Cristòfor press covered the trial in detail from the first hearing, and the interior provincial press followed the proceedings closely, particularly after the prosecution's witnesses testified.

=== Sant Cristòfor press ===

''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', the newspaper of record, provided the most extensive coverage, with daily trial reports and editorial commentary that generally supported the defence. The newspaper's cultural critic, [[Eulàlia Camps i Verdaguer]], published a series of essays arguing that the trial was a test of the Twelve Guarantees' commitment to freedom of expression and that a conviction would establish a precedent that could be used against any film that challenged the social norms of the interior. ''[[La Veu de la Badia]]'', the centre-left daily, editorially supported the defence and published opinion pieces by feminist and civil liberties writers. ''[[La Voz del Istmo]]'', the Creole-language weekly, published a special edition on the trial that included interviews with Riera i Sola, Vives i Cardó, and members of the underground film community.

=== Television ===

[[Telebadia]], the public broadcaster, covered the trial in its evening news programme and broadcast a documentary on the underground film community in May 1988 that included excerpts from ''El Desig'' and interviews with Riera i Sola and other underground filmmakers. The documentary was controversial: the Ministry of Justice issued a statement criticising the broadcast, and several BPP members of the National Assembly called for the documentary to be withdrawn. Telebadia's editorial independence, guaranteed by the public broadcasting statute, prevented the government from acting on the calls.

[[Canal Nord]], the commercial Volisanian-language broadcaster, covered the trial in its news programming and broadcast a panel discussion on the Public Decency Act in June 1988 that included Vives i Cardó, a representative of the Archdiocese of Sant Bart, and a professor of constitutional law. The panel discussion was the most-watched current affairs programme of the week.

=== Interior provincial press ===

The interior provincial press generally supported the prosecution. Newspapers in [[Sant Bart]], [[Portblanc]], and [[Novara]] published editorials arguing that the film was obscene and that the Public Decency Act was a necessary protection of the moral standards of the interior provinces. The [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] Provincial Assembly passed a resolution in May 1988 supporting the prosecution and calling on the government to "defend the moral standards of the Balboan people." The [[Nurra Province|Nurra]] Provincial Assembly passed a similar resolution in June. The [[Jala Province|Jala]] Provincial Assembly, divided between the conservative establishment of Sant Bart and the BPP-Socialist working-class districts, did not pass a resolution but debated the matter extensively.

=== The Balboan Catholic Church ===

The [[Balboan Catholic Church]] through the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] issued a statement in April 1988 supporting the prosecution and arguing that the film's content was "contrary to the moral values of the Balboan people." The Archdiocese's testimony at the trial, provided by a representative who was not a party to the case, was the most visible intervention by the Church in a cultural matter since reunification. The Church's position was criticised by the Sant Cristòfor press and by civil liberties organisations, which argued that the Church was seeking to impose the moral standards of the interior on the entire country.

=== Demonstrations ===

Demonstrations were held outside the courthouse during the trial. The first demonstration, organised by the defence committee on 14 March 1988 — the opening day of the trial — drew approximately 500 people and was attended by members of the underground film community, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups. Subsequent demonstrations were held at weekly intervals throughout the trial, with attendance growing to approximately 2,000 by June 1988. A counter-demonstration in support of the prosecution, organised by conservative interior provincial groups, drew approximately 300 people in July 1988.

=== Adonian Community intervention ===

The [[Adonian Community]]'s human rights directorate filed an amicus brief with the court in July 1988, arguing that the Public Decency Act was incompatible with the [[Twelve Guarantees]] and that the AC retained the authority to challenge Balboan laws that violated the Guarantees. The brief was significant because it represented the first intervention by the AC in a domestic Balboan court case and because it signalled that the international community was monitoring the trial's outcome. The government's decision not to appeal the acquittal was influenced, according to subsequent accounts by Ministry of Justice officials, by the AC's intervention and by the government's reluctance to provoke an international confrontation over the Public Decency Act.

== Ruling ==

The court delivered its ruling on 22 September 1988. Judge Tarradellas i Bayarri ruled that the film, while explicit, was a legitimate artistic expression and that the Public Decency Act's definition of obscenity was too vague to support a prosecution. The ruling held that the Act's standard for "gratuitous" content was unconstitutionally vague under the Twelve Guarantees' provisions on freedom of expression, as it provided no objective standard by which a court could distinguish between artistic and non-artistic depictions of sexuality. The court further held that the government's selective prosecution of ''El Desig'' — the first prosecution under the Act — demonstrated the statute's potential for arbitrary application.

The ruling effectively gutted the Public Decency Act and established a precedent that protected artistic expression from obscenity prosecution. The government did not appeal the ruling.

== Aftermath ==

=== Amendment of the Public Decency Act ===

The Public Decency Act was amended in 1990 to narrow its scope and to exempt artistic works from its provisions. The amendment, introduced by the Muntaner i Ferrer administration, redefined "obscene material" to exclude works of artistic, scientific, or educational merit and to require that any prosecution under the Act be preceded by a judicial determination of artistic merit. The amendment effectively rendered the Act unenforceable against films, books, and other artistic works.

=== Cultural Defence Coalition ===

The defence committee that had been organised to support Riera i Sola during the trial — which included representatives of the underground film community, the mainstream industry, feminist organisations, LGBT organisations, and civil liberties groups — reconstituted itself after the trial as the [[Cultural Defence Coalition]], a permanent advocacy organisation dedicated to defending artistic freedom and freedom of expression in Balboa. The Coalition became a significant force in Balboan cultural life and played a role in the subsequent campaigns for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] and the first [[Pride parade|Pride march]] in Sant Cristòfor in 1993.

=== Impact on cinema ===

The trial's outcome removed the legal threat that had constrained the underground film community and is widely regarded as a catalyst for the production of more confrontational films in the late 1980s and early 1990s. The first significant film produced in the aftermath of the trial was ''[[Els Amagats (film)|Els Amagats]]'' ("The Hidden Ones", 1989), directed by [[Adrià Pons i Riera]], which depicted the lives of three LGBT individuals in Sant Cristòfor and was the first Balboan film to depict LGBT lives with frankness. The trial also contributed to the partial convergence of the mainstream and underground film industries, as the mainstream's support for the defence — driven by self-interest as much as by solidarity — established a precedent for cooperation that would continue in the post-trial years.

=== Film preservation ===

''El Desig'' is preserved at the [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]] as a cultural artefact of foundational significance. The film was added to the national archive in 1995, following the closure of the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre and the dissolution of several underground production companies during the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]].

== Significance ==

The ''El Desig'' trial is widely regarded as a landmark in the development of Balboan civil liberties. The trial established that the Twelve Guarantees' protections for freedom of expression extended to artistic content that challenged social norms, and it removed the legal constraints that had inhibited the underground film community. The trial is also regarded as a turning point in the relationship between the Sant Cristòfor cultural establishment and the conservative interior: the prosecution, driven by the interior's moral standards, was defeated in the capital's courts, and the interior's attempt to impose its norms on the entire country was rebuffed. The trial's outcome did not resolve the underlying cultural tension between the permissive capital and the conservative interior, but it established that the legal framework of the reunified state would not permit the interior's standards to be enforced against the capital's artistic community.

